\begin_layout Subsection
Functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A 
\emph on
function
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 from a set 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 to a set 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is a rule that assigns to each element of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 a unique element of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The notation 
\begin_inset Formula $f:A\to B$
\end_inset

 denotes a function 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 The result of applying 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 to an element 
\begin_inset Formula $a\in A$
\end_inset

 is denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $f(a)$
\end_inset

;
 it is called the 
\emph on
value
\emph default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

,
 or the 
\emph on
image
\emph default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 under 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

.
 The set 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is called the 
\emph on
domain
\emph default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is called the 
\emph on
codomain
\emph default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The graph of a function 
\begin_inset Formula $f:A\to B$
\end_inset

 is the set of all ordered pairs 
\begin_inset Formula $(a,f(a))$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 ranged over all elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 It is a subset of the Cartesian product 
\begin_inset Formula $A\times B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Minisec
Indexed family
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Intuitively,
 an indexed family is a set whose elements are labelled with the elements of another (index) set 
\begin_inset Formula $I\subseteq\mathbb{N}$
\end_inset

.
 These labels allow the same element to occure several times in the family.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An indexed family is a function that has an index set 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 as its domain.
 Generally,
 the usual functional notation is not used for indexed families.
 Instead,
 the element 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 of the index set 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 is written as a subscript of the same name of the family,
 such as in 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 For
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $I=\{0,1\}$
\end_inset

,
 we have an ordered pair.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $I=\{0,...,n-1\}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $n\in\mathbb{N}$
\end_inset

,
 we have an 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-tuple.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $I=\mathbb{N}$
\end_inset

,
 we have a sequence.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The above notations
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bigcup\nolimits_{A\in\mathcal{S}}A\text{ and }\bigcap\nolimits_{A\in\mathcal{S}}
\]

\end_inset

are commonly replaced with a notation involving indexed families,
 namely
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bigcup\nolimits_{i\in I}A_{i}=\{x|(\exists i\in I\}x\in A_{i})\text{ and }\bigcap\nolimits_{i\in I}A_{i}=\{x|(\forall i\in I\}x\in A_{i}).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

