\begin_layout Minisec
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "minisec:Cartesian-product"

\end_inset

Cartesian product
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given sets 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

,
 their 
\emph on
Cartesian product
\emph default
 (or simply 
\emph on
product
\emph default
),
 denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $A\times B$
\end_inset

,
 is the set of all ordered pairs 
\begin_inset Formula $(a,b)$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $a\in A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b\in B$
\end_inset

;
 that is,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A\times B=\{(a,b)|a\in A\wedge b\in B\}.
\]

\end_inset

The definition makes sense even if 
\begin_inset Formula $A=B$
\end_inset

,
 in which case we write 
\begin_inset Formula $A\times A=A^{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given an (ordered) indexed family of sets 
\begin_inset Formula $(A_{i})_{i\in I}$
\end_inset

,
 the product
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\prod\nolimits_{i\in I}A_{i}
\]

\end_inset

is the set of all indexed families of elements 
\begin_inset Formula $(a_{i})_{i\in I}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}\in A_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in I$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Minisec
Set exponentiation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given two sets 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

,
 the 
\emph on
set exponentiation
\emph default
,
 denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $B^{A}$
\end_inset

,
 is the set that has as elements all functions 
\begin_inset Formula $A\rightarrow B$
\end_inset

,
 i.e.:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
B^{A}=\{f|f:A\rightarrow B\}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Equivalently,
 
\begin_inset Formula $B^{A}$
\end_inset

 can be viewed as the Cartesian product of a family,
 indexed by 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 of sets that are all equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

.
 This explains the terminology and the notation,
 since exponentiation with integer exponents is a product where all factors are equal to the base:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
B^{A}=\prod\nolimits_{a\in A}B=\{(b_{a})_{a\in A}|(\forall a\in A)b_{a}\in B\}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Minisec
Power Set
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The power set of a set 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

 is the set that has all subsets of 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

 as elements,
 including 
\begin_inset Formula $\emptyset$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

.
 It is often denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{P}(E)$
\end_inset

.
 For example:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{P}(\{0,1,2\})=\{\emptyset,\{0\},\{1\},\{2\},\{0,1\},\{0,2\},\{1,2\},\{0,1,2\}\}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There is a natural one-to one correspondence between the subsets of 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

 and the functions from 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\{0,1\}$
\end_inset

;
 this correspondence associates to each subset the function that takes the value 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 on the subset an 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 everywhere else.
 Because of the correspondence,
 the power set of 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

 is commonly identified with set exponentiation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{P}(E)=\{0,1\}^{E}=2^{E}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In particular,
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

 has 
\begin_inset Formula $n\in\mathbb{N}$
\end_inset

 elements,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{E}$
\end_inset

 has 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{n}$
\end_inset

 elements:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\vert2^{E}\vert=2^{\vert E\vert}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Cardinality
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Informally,
 the cardinality of a set 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

,
 often denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $|S|$
\end_inset

,
 is the number of its members.
 This number is the natural number 
\begin_inset Formula $n\in\mathbb{N}$
\end_inset

 when there is a one-to-one correspondence between the set 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 and the set 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{N}_{n}=\{1,2,...,n\}$
\end_inset

 of the first 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 natural numbers.
 Also,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\vert\emptyset\vert=0.
\]

\end_inset

A set with the cardinality of a natural number is called a finite set,
 which applies in both cases.
 Otherwise,
 one has an infinite set.
\end_layout

